1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:02,157
On the afternoon of April eighteenth,

2
00:00:02,177 --> 00:00:05,280
1942, a restaurant owner in Detroit told a

3
00:00:05,300 --> 00:00:08,180
young man at one of his tables that it was

4
00:00:08,200 --> 00:00:10,500
his birthday, and they were going on a

5
00:00:10,520 --> 00:00:11,080
pleasure trip.

6
00:00:11,100 --> 00:00:13,400
He gave him a necktie and a wallet,

7
00:00:13,420 --> 00:00:14,850
bought him a traveling bag,

8
00:00:14,870 --> 00:00:16,880
and drove him from bar to bar.

9
00:00:16,900 --> 00:00:19,490
At each stop, he introduced his guest as a

10
00:00:19,510 --> 00:00:21,810
friend from Milwaukee, or as one of the

11
00:00:21,830 --> 00:00:22,630
Meyers boys.

12
00:00:23,750 --> 00:00:26,195
The guest was not from Milwaukee.

13
00:00:26,215 --> 00:00:29,050
He was Lieutenant Hans Peter Krug,

14
00:00:29,070 --> 00:00:31,542
a bomber pilot of the German air force.

15
00:00:31,562 --> 00:00:34,035
A few days earlier, he had been behind

16
00:00:34,055 --> 00:00:37,170
barbed wire, in a prisoner of war camp in Canada.

17
00:00:38,475 --> 00:00:40,697
The restaurant owner was Max Stephan,

18
00:00:40,717 --> 00:00:43,312
an American citizen for almost seven years.

19
00:00:43,332 --> 00:00:45,927
Within four months, a federal judge would

20
00:00:45,947 --> 00:00:47,422
sentence him to hang.

21
00:00:47,442 --> 00:00:50,411
This is the documented story of the German

22
00:00:50,431 --> 00:00:52,279
prisoner who escaped into America,

23
00:00:52,299 --> 00:00:55,268
and of the man who spent one weekend

24
00:00:55,288 --> 00:00:56,088
helping him.

25
00:00:57,700 --> 00:01:00,620
Krug testified that in August 1940,

26
00:01:00,640 --> 00:01:02,355
during the Battle of Britain,

27
00:01:02,375 --> 00:01:05,132
he was shot down while flying a bomber

28
00:01:05,152 --> 00:01:05,826
over England.

29
00:01:05,846 --> 00:01:09,307
In January 1941 he was shipped across the

30
00:01:09,327 --> 00:01:11,360
Atlantic to a camp in northern Ontario,

31
00:01:11,380 --> 00:01:13,707
and later to a camp for officers at

32
00:01:13,727 --> 00:01:14,900
Bowmanville, east of Toronto.

33
00:01:16,350 --> 00:01:19,329
Bowmanville had been a training school for boys.

34
00:01:19,349 --> 00:01:21,953
The Canadians ringed it with two barbed

35
00:01:21,973 --> 00:01:24,202
wire fences and nine guard towers.

36
00:01:24,222 --> 00:01:27,200
For an officer, trying to escape was a

37
00:01:27,220 --> 00:01:29,928
duty, and Krug knew it could be done.

38
00:01:29,948 --> 00:01:32,158
The year before, another German pilot,

39
00:01:32,178 --> 00:01:34,908
Franz von Werra, had jumped from a prison

40
00:01:34,928 --> 00:01:36,922
train in Canada, crossed the frozen Saint

41
00:01:36,942 --> 00:01:38,648
Lawrence River into the neutral United

42
00:01:38,668 --> 00:01:40,970
States, and made it all the way home.

43
00:01:42,650 --> 00:01:44,480
In the middle of April 1942,

44
00:01:44,500 --> 00:01:46,007
Krug got out.

45
00:01:46,027 --> 00:01:48,158
In Toronto, military police stopped him.

46
00:01:48,178 --> 00:01:51,025
He showed them forged papers that said he

47
00:01:51,045 --> 00:01:53,893
was a carpenter from the crew of the

48
00:01:53,913 --> 00:01:57,017
French liner Normandie, and they let him go.

49
00:01:57,037 --> 00:01:58,922
A Catholic priest, who believed his

50
00:01:58,942 --> 00:02:01,145
stories, bought him a bus ticket to

51
00:02:01,165 --> 00:02:03,070
Windsor, across the river from Detroit.

52
00:02:04,400 --> 00:02:07,056
In Windsor, he stole a rowboat and rowed

53
00:02:07,076 --> 00:02:08,393
across the Detroit River.

54
00:02:08,413 --> 00:02:11,069
At around nine at night, he landed on

55
00:02:11,089 --> 00:02:13,076
Belle Isle, the city's island park.

56
00:02:13,096 --> 00:02:16,086
He was now inside a country that had been

57
00:02:16,106 --> 00:02:18,427
at war with Germany for four months.

58
00:02:18,447 --> 00:02:20,768
He spent the night in a doorway,

59
00:02:20,788 --> 00:02:22,460
and in a vegetable cart.

60
00:02:24,150 --> 00:02:26,786
Other prisoners had given Krug an address.

61
00:02:26,806 --> 00:02:29,175
Margareta Bertelmann was born in Germany

62
00:02:29,195 --> 00:02:31,963
and had never become an American citizen.

63
00:02:31,983 --> 00:02:34,750
She knitted for the German prisoners in

64
00:02:34,770 --> 00:02:37,935
Canada, and her packages had to carry her

65
00:02:37,955 --> 00:02:39,150
name and address.

66
00:02:40,475 --> 00:02:43,002
At about half past nine on Saturday

67
00:02:43,022 --> 00:02:46,277
morning, a man in coveralls knocked at her door.

68
00:02:46,297 --> 00:02:49,188
He showed her the shoulder boards of a

69
00:02:49,208 --> 00:02:49,916
German officer.

70
00:02:49,936 --> 00:02:52,827
She let him in, and telephoned a friend:

71
00:02:52,847 --> 00:02:53,647
Max Stephan.

72
00:02:54,675 --> 00:02:58,735
Stephan was born in Cologne in 1892.

73
00:02:58,755 --> 00:03:01,845
He was wounded in the German army in the

74
00:03:01,865 --> 00:03:04,609
First World War, and later became a policeman.

75
00:03:04,629 --> 00:03:06,948
In 1928 he moved to Windsor,

76
00:03:06,968 --> 00:03:10,427
and in 1933 he crossed to Detroit.

77
00:03:10,447 --> 00:03:13,655
In 1935 he became an American citizen,

78
00:03:13,675 --> 00:03:16,538
and swore to renounce all allegiance to

79
00:03:16,558 --> 00:03:19,421
any foreign state, and particularly to Germany.

80
00:03:19,441 --> 00:03:21,893
His restaurant on Jefferson Avenue hosted

81
00:03:21,913 --> 00:03:24,364
meetings of the German American Bund,

82
00:03:24,384 --> 00:03:25,600
a pro-Nazi group.

83
00:03:25,620 --> 00:03:28,915
Stephan denied that he was ever a member.

84
00:03:30,650 --> 00:03:34,119
Stephan arrived within about a quarter of

85
00:03:34,139 --> 00:03:35,116
an hour.

86
00:03:35,136 --> 00:03:35,615
Mrs.

87
00:03:35,635 --> 00:03:37,110
Bertelmann was frightened.

88
00:03:37,130 --> 00:03:40,101
She testified that Stephan told her:

89
00:03:40,121 --> 00:03:42,593
what are you shaking for?

90
00:03:42,613 --> 00:03:43,590
You're crazy.

91
00:03:43,610 --> 00:03:46,898
Krug remembered him sitting down in the

92
00:03:46,918 --> 00:03:47,574
kitchen, smiling.

93
00:03:47,594 --> 00:03:50,616
She heard Stephan ask the pilot why he did

94
00:03:50,636 --> 00:03:51,630
not give up.

95
00:03:51,650 --> 00:03:54,230
Krug answered that he had to try.

96
00:03:55,525 --> 00:03:57,292
Then the help began.

97
00:03:57,312 --> 00:04:00,367
One of Krug's shoes had a broken sole.

98
00:04:00,387 --> 00:04:00,743
Mrs.

99
00:04:00,763 --> 00:04:02,625
Bertelmann found him another pair,

100
00:04:02,645 --> 00:04:05,260
socks she had darned for her husband,

101
00:04:05,280 --> 00:04:08,647
and underwear she had meant to send to the

102
00:04:08,667 --> 00:04:09,776
prisoners in Canada.

103
00:04:09,796 --> 00:04:12,787
Twenty dollars of hers lay on the table

104
00:04:12,807 --> 00:04:13,540
near Stephan.

105
00:04:13,560 --> 00:04:16,174
When the men left, it was gone.

106
00:04:16,194 --> 00:04:19,205
Stephan walked the pilot out to his car.

107
00:04:20,875 --> 00:04:22,942
At the restaurant, Stephan went in through

108
00:04:22,962 --> 00:04:25,425
the back, and told Krug to come in the

109
00:04:25,445 --> 00:04:26,435
front, like any customer.

110
00:04:26,455 --> 00:04:29,257
Krug ate, and did not pay.

111
00:04:29,277 --> 00:04:31,821
Then the escaped enemy officer went for a

112
00:04:31,841 --> 00:04:33,423
walk, alone, through downtown Detroit.

113
00:04:33,443 --> 00:04:35,346
When he came back, Stephan said:

114
00:04:35,366 --> 00:04:38,551
now I have nothing to do, and it is your

115
00:04:38,571 --> 00:04:40,794
birthday today, and we will take a

116
00:04:40,814 --> 00:04:41,614
pleasure trip.

117
00:04:42,675 --> 00:04:44,966
At Haller's Cafe on Randolph Street,

118
00:04:44,986 --> 00:04:48,047
Stephan introduced the pilot as one of the

119
00:04:48,067 --> 00:04:48,817
Meyers boys.

120
00:04:48,837 --> 00:04:51,513
At the Progressive Club, they had beer.

121
00:04:51,533 --> 00:04:54,594
Then they went to the shop of Theodore

122
00:04:54,614 --> 00:04:55,750
Donay, an importer.

123
00:04:55,770 --> 00:04:57,735
Here Krug gave his real rank,

124
00:04:57,755 --> 00:05:00,115
and told the story of his escape,

125
00:05:00,135 --> 00:05:03,515
and of his plan to get back to Germany and

126
00:05:03,535 --> 00:05:04,195
fly again.

127
00:05:04,215 --> 00:05:05,555
Stephan bought him candy.

128
00:05:05,575 --> 00:05:07,935
Twenty dollars came out of Donay's cash

129
00:05:07,955 --> 00:05:09,875
drawer for Krug.

130
00:05:09,895 --> 00:05:12,178
A clerk named Dietrich Rintelen watched

131
00:05:12,198 --> 00:05:14,255
the three men talking close together.

132
00:05:15,925 --> 00:05:18,103
Back at the restaurant, Krug ate dinner

133
00:05:18,123 --> 00:05:20,404
and sat in a booth until nine.

134
00:05:20,424 --> 00:05:23,363
When a young couple sat down at his table,

135
00:05:23,382 --> 00:05:25,993
Stephan pointed him out as a friend from

136
00:05:26,012 --> 00:05:26,321
Milwaukee.

137
00:05:26,341 --> 00:05:28,951
A waitress offered the pilot a spare room

138
00:05:28,971 --> 00:05:29,938
in her house.

139
00:05:29,957 --> 00:05:30,924
Stephan said no:

140
00:05:30,944 --> 00:05:33,245
he could sleep at the Field Hotel.

141
00:05:34,550 --> 00:05:36,030
At eight the next morning,

142
00:05:36,050 --> 00:05:38,780
Sunday, Krug was back at the restaurant

143
00:05:38,800 --> 00:05:39,974
door, as arranged.

144
00:05:39,994 --> 00:05:42,760
Stephan had looked up the bus schedule.

145
00:05:42,780 --> 00:05:44,750
They had breakfast together downtown.

146
00:05:44,770 --> 00:05:47,934
Then Stephan bought him a bus ticket to

147
00:05:47,954 --> 00:05:49,470
Chicago, and Krug was gone.

148
00:05:51,200 --> 00:05:53,460
The next day, the clerk, Rintelen,

149
00:05:53,480 --> 00:05:56,086
found a slip in Donay's cash drawer for

150
00:05:56,106 --> 00:05:57,727
twenty dollars and sixty-eight cents.

151
00:05:57,747 --> 00:06:00,900
He went to the FBI.

152
00:06:00,920 --> 00:06:02,765
On Monday morning, April twentieth,

153
00:06:02,785 --> 00:06:04,256
agents arrested Max Stephan.

154
00:06:04,276 --> 00:06:06,866
That same day he signed a statement.

155
00:06:06,886 --> 00:06:09,477
He admitted he had checked the bus

156
00:06:09,497 --> 00:06:10,968
schedules, bought the bag,

157
00:06:10,988 --> 00:06:12,480
and bought the ticket.

158
00:06:14,225 --> 00:06:16,008
Krug was heading for Mexico,

159
00:06:16,028 --> 00:06:17,410
which was still neutral.

160
00:06:17,430 --> 00:06:19,164
According to one later account,

161
00:06:19,184 --> 00:06:22,320
people in Chicago gave him a new suit and

162
00:06:22,340 --> 00:06:24,774
a hundred dollars and sent him east,

163
00:06:24,794 --> 00:06:26,528
to Philadelphia and New York,

164
00:06:26,548 --> 00:06:30,034
where he hoped to stow away on a Swedish ship.

165
00:06:30,054 --> 00:06:32,138
When that failed, he turned south:

166
00:06:32,158 --> 00:06:33,190
Pittsburgh, Cincinnati, Nashville,

167
00:06:33,210 --> 00:06:35,665
Dallas, toward the border crossing at Laredo.

168
00:06:36,875 --> 00:06:40,315
On May first, 1942, twelve days after he

169
00:06:40,335 --> 00:06:42,773
left Detroit, Krug was arrested in San

170
00:06:42,793 --> 00:06:44,780
Antonio, Texas, about a hundred fifty

171
00:06:44,800 --> 00:06:46,118
miles from the border.

172
00:06:46,138 --> 00:06:48,794
One account says the woman who ran his

173
00:06:48,814 --> 00:06:52,895
hotel heard his accent, and called the FBI.

174
00:06:52,915 --> 00:06:54,562
On him, agents found a revolver,

175
00:06:54,582 --> 00:06:57,062
a Greyhound bus map, and an atlas of the

176
00:06:57,082 --> 00:06:57,915
world at war.

177
00:06:59,600 --> 00:07:02,515
Hiding an escaped prisoner was a crime.

178
00:07:02,535 --> 00:07:04,611
But the prosecutors wanted more.

179
00:07:04,631 --> 00:07:07,964
In June, a grand jury indicted Max Stephan

180
00:07:07,984 --> 00:07:08,803
for treason.

181
00:07:08,823 --> 00:07:11,318
The Constitution defines treason in one

182
00:07:11,338 --> 00:07:11,737
sentence:

183
00:07:11,757 --> 00:07:14,253
waging war against the United States,

184
00:07:14,273 --> 00:07:16,349
or adhering to their enemies,

185
00:07:16,369 --> 00:07:18,445
giving them aid and comfort.

186
00:07:18,465 --> 00:07:21,799
And it adds a rule found nowhere else.

187
00:07:21,819 --> 00:07:24,733
No one can be convicted unless two

188
00:07:24,753 --> 00:07:27,668
witnesses testify to the same overt act.

189
00:07:27,688 --> 00:07:29,764
The indictment listed twelve acts,

190
00:07:29,784 --> 00:07:31,880
all from that one weekend.

191
00:07:33,650 --> 00:07:35,630
The trial opened on June twenty-ninth,

192
00:07:35,650 --> 00:07:38,799
1942, before Judge Arthur Tuttle.

193
00:07:38,819 --> 00:07:41,550
The government's main witness was Krug

194
00:07:41,570 --> 00:07:43,768
himself, brought from Canada under guard,

195
00:07:43,788 --> 00:07:46,725
in the uniform of the German air force.

196
00:07:46,745 --> 00:07:50,052
Under the rules of war, he could not be

197
00:07:50,072 --> 00:07:51,161
forced to testify.

198
00:07:51,181 --> 00:07:52,290
He chose to.

199
00:07:53,575 --> 00:07:56,753
Then, in the afternoon, he turned to the judge.

200
00:07:56,773 --> 00:07:59,596
Someone on Stephan's side had told him he

201
00:07:59,616 --> 00:08:01,373
was testifying against the man.

202
00:08:01,393 --> 00:08:03,860
I don't intend to testify against Stephan,

203
00:08:03,880 --> 00:08:04,571
he said.

204
00:08:04,591 --> 00:08:07,414
I never can testify against a man who

205
00:08:07,434 --> 00:08:08,125
helped me.

206
00:08:08,145 --> 00:08:10,968
The judge told him to tell the truth,

207
00:08:10,988 --> 00:08:13,475
no matter whom it helped or harmed.

208
00:08:14,775 --> 00:08:16,446
Stephan never took the stand.

209
00:08:16,466 --> 00:08:19,151
His lawyer compared him to the Good Samaritan.

210
00:08:19,171 --> 00:08:21,856
The prosecutor told the jury it was Max

211
00:08:21,876 --> 00:08:24,561
Stephan who tried to get this pilot back

212
00:08:24,581 --> 00:08:27,266
to Germany, so he could climb into a

213
00:08:27,286 --> 00:08:29,315
bomber and bomb the United States.

214
00:08:31,050 --> 00:08:33,250
Judge Tuttle told the jury that helping

215
00:08:33,270 --> 00:08:36,179
Krug out of friendship or sympathy was not

216
00:08:36,199 --> 00:08:36,563
treason.

217
00:08:36,583 --> 00:08:39,252
They could convict only if Stephan helped

218
00:08:39,272 --> 00:08:42,709
him on purpose, to help Germany win the war.

219
00:08:42,729 --> 00:08:45,398
On July second, after an hour and

220
00:08:45,418 --> 00:08:48,086
twenty-three minutes, the jury found him guilty.

221
00:08:48,106 --> 00:08:50,775
By most accounts, he was the first

222
00:08:50,795 --> 00:08:53,080
American convicted of treason in a

223
00:08:53,100 --> 00:08:55,384
civilian federal court since the Whiskey

224
00:08:55,404 --> 00:08:57,325
Rebellion of the seventeen nineties.

225
00:08:58,025 --> 00:08:59,958
On August sixth, Judge Tuttle sentenced

226
00:08:59,978 --> 00:09:01,586
him to death by hanging.

227
00:09:01,606 --> 00:09:03,864
The life of a traitor, he said,

228
00:09:03,884 --> 00:09:06,468
was less valuable than the lives of the

229
00:09:06,488 --> 00:09:07,465
country's loyal sons.

230
00:09:09,275 --> 00:09:12,311
Appeals pushed the date back again and again.

231
00:09:12,331 --> 00:09:16,083
In February 1943, the Court of Appeals

232
00:09:16,103 --> 00:09:18,858
went through the twelve acts one by one.

233
00:09:18,878 --> 00:09:21,286
Some had never been proved by two

234
00:09:21,306 --> 00:09:21,633
witnesses:

235
00:09:21,653 --> 00:09:24,061
the drive to the house, the twenty

236
00:09:24,081 --> 00:09:25,102
dollars, the candy.

237
00:09:25,122 --> 00:09:26,143
But others had:

238
00:09:26,163 --> 00:09:28,918
the walk to the car, the drinks at

239
00:09:28,938 --> 00:09:30,305
Haller's Cafe, the dinner,

240
00:09:30,325 --> 00:09:30,999
the hotel.

241
00:09:31,019 --> 00:09:32,040
The conviction stood.

242
00:09:32,060 --> 00:09:34,835
The Supreme Court declined to hear the case.

243
00:09:36,150 --> 00:09:38,068
Some people told the President that

244
00:09:38,088 --> 00:09:40,652
Stephan had only felt sorry for a young

245
00:09:40,672 --> 00:09:41,944
man far from home.

246
00:09:41,964 --> 00:09:44,204
Judge Tuttle answered that there should be

247
00:09:44,224 --> 00:09:45,496
no trifling with traitors,

248
00:09:45,516 --> 00:09:47,757
and set the hanging for July second,

249
00:09:47,777 --> 00:09:51,370
1943, at the federal prison at Milan,

250
00:09:51,390 --> 00:09:51,922
Michigan.

251
00:09:51,942 --> 00:09:54,150
A scaffold was built.

252
00:09:55,775 --> 00:09:58,543
At four fifty-five in the afternoon on

253
00:09:58,563 --> 00:10:01,331
July first, about nine hours before the

254
00:10:01,351 --> 00:10:03,322
hanging, word reached the prison.

255
00:10:03,342 --> 00:10:05,314
President Roosevelt had commuted the

256
00:10:05,334 --> 00:10:07,305
sentence to life in prison.

257
00:10:07,325 --> 00:10:09,715
He believed death was too harsh.

258
00:10:10,950 --> 00:10:14,095
The White House statement gave Stephan no comfort.

259
00:10:14,115 --> 00:10:16,864
The man was properly convicted of treason,

260
00:10:16,884 --> 00:10:17,655
it said.

261
00:10:17,675 --> 00:10:18,842
He was guilty.

262
00:10:18,862 --> 00:10:21,216
The President believed he was pro-German

263
00:10:21,236 --> 00:10:24,381
or pro-Nazi, and hoped that none of his

264
00:10:24,401 --> 00:10:27,170
successors would ever commute the life sentence.

265
00:10:28,950 --> 00:10:31,570
Max Stephan spent eight years in the

266
00:10:31,590 --> 00:10:33,079
federal penitentiary in Atlanta,

267
00:10:33,099 --> 00:10:36,096
and was moved to the prison hospital at

268
00:10:36,116 --> 00:10:36,850
Springfield, Missouri.

269
00:10:36,870 --> 00:10:41,590
He died there of cancer in January 1952.

270
00:10:41,610 --> 00:10:43,931
His wife Agnes was interned,

271
00:10:43,951 --> 00:10:46,272
and later deported to Germany.

272
00:10:46,292 --> 00:10:48,145
So was Margareta Bertelmann.

273
00:10:48,165 --> 00:10:51,910
Theodore Donay was later sent to prison too.

274
00:10:53,175 --> 00:10:56,378
Hans Peter Krug was sent back to Canada.

275
00:10:56,398 --> 00:10:59,158
In August 1943 he escaped again,

276
00:10:59,178 --> 00:11:00,602
and was caught again.

277
00:11:00,622 --> 00:11:02,769
After the war he went home,

278
00:11:02,789 --> 00:11:04,955
and sold steel in West Germany.

279
00:11:06,250 --> 00:11:08,554
If your family lived in Detroit during the

280
00:11:08,574 --> 00:11:10,297
war, or remembers the Stephan case,

281
00:11:10,317 --> 00:11:11,770
tell us in the comments.

282
00:11:13,525 --> 00:11:16,324
The old boys' school at Bowmanville still stands.

283
00:11:16,344 --> 00:11:18,790
In 2013, Canada made it a national

284
00:11:18,810 --> 00:11:19,495
historic site.

285
00:11:19,515 --> 00:11:21,609
And in the federal law books,

286
00:11:21,629 --> 00:11:23,370
the case is still there:

287
00:11:23,390 --> 00:11:26,541
one weekend in Detroit, written up as a list.

288
00:11:26,561 --> 00:11:28,655
A necktie, a wallet, a bag,

289
00:11:28,675 --> 00:11:31,474
two bars, a box of candy, a dinner,

290
00:11:31,494 --> 00:11:34,292
a hotel room, breakfast, and a bus ticket

291
00:11:34,312 --> 00:11:34,997
to Chicago.

292
00:11:35,017 --> 00:11:37,463
Twelve acts, each one sorted by whether

293
00:11:37,483 --> 00:11:39,245
two people had seen it.

